Airlocks and transfers have a sequence of physical states (@cm-airlock-state: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/Sterile-Products-WHO.lingua#r_6BVEG1GSVGGGD566CJKD254DSB", #source "../sources/CONTAM-Multizone-Airflow-and-Contaminants-NIST.lingua#r_5HECDC480RSYAJ3SQ2QNFHCN46", #clean-manufacturing "Observe-flow-where-the-process-and-the-boundary-actually-are.lingua#r_1HV5QEQ2V7PBHXCAC15FVHBKBR") { r_7WTWHCP2R46ZH8RK87B41Q6NMG
**Read:** [Sterile pharmaceutical products — WHO TRS 1044, Annex 2](../sources/Sterile-Products-WHO.lingua#r_6BVEG1GSVGGGD566CJKD254DSB), airlocks and material/personnel transfer; [Multizone airflow and contaminants — Dols and Polidoro, CONTAM 3.4](../sources/CONTAM-Multizone-Airflow-and-Contaminants-NIST.lingua#r_5HECDC480RSYAJ3SQ2QNFHCN46), transient airflow and contaminants.

An airlock's condition changes during entry, closing, purging, waiting and exit. The item and person can carry material independent of the air. Interlocking doors can prevent a particular simultaneous opening but does not itself demonstrate adequate exchange, clean surfaces or readiness.

An ideal closed, mixed airlock with constant clean supply has the same exponential recovery balance already derived. For hypothetical V=10 m³ and Q=0.5 m³/s without an internal source, reducing the concentration gap by 99% takes approximately 92.10 s. Actual exposure during transfer must be measured under its own conditions.

**Work:** Draw the state sequence, including interruption and a door that does not seal as assumed. Account for air, surfaces and the object separately. Derive a condition for advancing to the next physical state and identify the observation supporting it. Evaluate throughput without replacing evidence by an arbitrary waiting time.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
